"""Reference solution for the FlagGems ``argwhere`` operator.

This file is the reference-as-solution artifact for the project ``pytest-review``
probe. It is injected by the existing FlagGems mechanism
``--override argwhere:<this file>:run`` and is therefore the candidate observed
by the frozen master suites, never by modifying Gems sources.

Contract taken from the frozen master tests
(``tests/test_argwhere.py`` lines 24-120, ``benchmark/test_argwhere.py``
lines 23-31) and from the master implementation's documented behaviour
(``src/flag_gems/ops/argwhere.py`` lines 161-229):

* input: one tensor, any rank (0-element shapes and ranks 1..9 are exercised);
  dtypes float16/float32/bfloat16, int16/int32 and bool; contiguous, strided
  (``randn(8, 8)[::2, ::3]``) and -0.0 / NaN / inf element values;
* output: a NEW ``torch.int64`` tensor of shape ``(num_nonzero, input.ndim)``
  on the input device, holding the multi-dimensional coordinates of every
  non-zero element in row-major flat-index order; ``-0.0`` is zero while NaN
  and inf are non-zero;
* no input mutation, no aliasing of the input storage, no ``out=`` argument and
  no autograd requirement (the operator returns indices, not a differentiable
  quantity).

The expected values of the frozen tests are computed with the native
``torch.argwhere``; this reference delegates to that same ATen operator so the
declared ABI (positional single tensor argument, int64 index tensor result) and
the oracle semantics stay exactly the ones the master suites assert. It does
not import, copy or otherwise consult the current FlagGems candidate, and it
performs no CPU round-trip and no dtype/device rewriting.

The ATen callable is bound once at import time, i.e. before any FlagGems
dispatch scope is entered, so instantiating this reference can never re-enter a
replaced ``flag_gems``/dispatch wrapper for the same operator.
"""

import torch

# Native ATen argwhere, bound before any Gems dispatch scope exists.
_NATIVE_ARGWHERE = torch.argwhere


def run(input):
    """Return the ``(num_nonzero, input.ndim)`` int64 index tensor of ``input``."""
    return _NATIVE_ARGWHERE(input)
